    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> was a distinct kingdom only in short interludes, under Ordoño II from 910, García II from 1065 and Alfonso VII, crowned at Compostela in 1111; otherwise it shared its kings with León and, from 1230, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>.</li><li>The tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-the-great">Saint James the Great</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> made the city the religious centre of the kingdom and the goal of the Way of St. James, a major international pilgrimage route.</li><li>From 1467 to 1469 the Irmandade, a brotherhood led by city dwellers with armies of armed peasants, governed the kingdom and demolished tens of the nobles' strongholds.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>From 1423 the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a>, in León but historically linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, represented the Kingdom of Galicia in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a>, its deputies sitting at the monarch's right, until 1640, usually against the will and advice of the Galician cities.</p></aside>
    </section>

