    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom's roots lay in the 5th century, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a> settled in the Roman province of Gallaecia, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Bracara Augusta</a> (Braga) their capital and founded a kingdom known first as the Kingdom of the Suebi and later as the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>. It lasted from 409 until 585, when the Visigothic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liuvigild">Leovigild</a> annexed it, and Galicia remained a distinct province of the Visigothic realm until the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalete">Guadalete</a> in 711. From the mid-8th century the north-west was free of Arab presence and was gradually joined to the Christian state usually called the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>, whose Galician noblemen conquered and resettled southern Galicia and northern Portugal down to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a> in the 9th century.</p><p>When Alfonso III was forced by his sons to abdicate in 910, his lands were partitioned. García I received León, Fruela II the Asturian heartland, and Ordoño II the western lands, Galicia with the newly acquired lands of Coimbra, where he had already served as governor; he was recognised as king at an assembly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucus-augusti">Lugo</a>. From Galicia Ordoño raided the Islamic south and returned with riches and Muslim serfs. On García's death in 914 he also acquired León, and the kingdom of Galicia, though tied to León by its dynasty, remained a distinct political entity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the Early and High <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the rural north became the leading part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>. The local economy was subsistence, based on grain, beans and above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>; fruits, salt, wine, honey, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, horses and iron came from particular districts, and long-range trade, generally in the hands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> merchants, was rare. Money was scarce, mostly old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a> and Visigothic coins known as solidos gallicianos. War and pillage against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a> brought riches and Muslim serfs, and the pilgrims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> brought wealth and continental innovations, from shipbuilding to Romanesque art. The basic unit was the villa, centred on a church, which later became the rural parishes.</p><p>Ferdinand II and Alfonso IX granted charters to towns such as Pontevedra, Noia, Betanzos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-coruna">A Coruña</a> and Baiona, which attracted peasants, artisans and merchants and broke the autarky of earlier centuries; salted and dried fish, fish oil and wine went out through the seaports to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. City councils and artisan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> arose, and Master Matthew's Portico da Gloria at Compostela showed the prosperity of the age. In 1348 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a>, known locally as A Mortaldade, reached the Galician ports and caused a severe and lasting economic crisis.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>'s nobles made and unmade kings. They crowned Ordoño IV at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> in 958, resented the lack of Leonese help when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> raided Galicia from 968 to 970, and in 982 crowned Bermudo, who defeated Ramiro III at the battle of Portela de Areas. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a>, who led a brief resurgence of the Cordoban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a>, later raided Santiago de Compostela itself. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Iria and Compostela were warlords in their own right, holding the fortresses of a Norman march. In 1071 García II defeated the rebel Count of Portugal at the Battle of Pedroso, only to be driven into exile by his brothers; in 1111 the Galician knights supporting the boy Alfonso VII were defeated by the Aragonese infantry of Alfonso the Battler at Viadangos.</p><p>After the union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, Galicia backed claimants against the crown: John of León in 1296, Ferdinand I of Portugal in 1369 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gaunt">John of Gaunt</a>, who landed at Coruña in 1386 and soon held the whole kingdom before plague decimated his army. The 15th century brought wars among noble houses holding castles across the country, and in 1467 the armies of the Irmandade, mostly armed peasants, demolished tens of strongholds before the nobles returned in 1469 with gunmen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. The Catholic Monarchs then subdued the rebellious nobility with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-hermandad">Santa Hermandad</a>, a Castilian police and military corps.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> was divided into bishoprics, and each bishopric into territories or counties whose rights of tax collection and government the king granted to a count, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> or a large monastery. In the Terra de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of the bishops of Iria-Compostela, a bishop's vicar ran each territory and justice was given by a council of churchmen, knights and peasants. Galicia and León kept the Visigothic legal code, the Liber Iudicum, and their own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a>, and public charters in the kingdom were written in Galician.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> the crown placed a meyrino maor in Galicia from 1231, replaced in 1251 by an adelantado mayor with greater powers. The kingdom lost its vote in the Cortes in the late 14th or early 15th century, and from 1423 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a> spoke for it until 1640. The Catholic Monarchs sent a Governor of the Kingdom, an office first established in 1475. In 1500 they created the Real <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> del Reino de Galicia, a permanent royal tribunal. The Junta, or General Assembly of the Kingdom, from 1599 seven deputies of the provincial capitals, could grant or deny armies, ships and taxes and petition the king; in 1623 Galicia regained its vote in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-castile">Council of Castile</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The shrine of Saint James at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> a reach far beyond its size. The Way of St. James drew pilgrims from all over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-gelmirez">Diego Gelmírez</a> won the elevation of Compostela into an archdiocese in 1120, with jurisdiction over the old see of Mérida; the rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Braga</a> that followed made Braga the centre of the movement for Portugal's independence. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a> Muslim sources, and among European Christians, the whole kingdom of León was known as Gallaecia or Galicia up to the 14th century.</p><p>Galicia gave the peninsula a language of culture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galician-portuguese">Galician-Portuguese</a>, which contemporaries held to be one language, flourished in the 13th and 14th centuries, and some 2,000 cantigas survive in its love songs, songs of a woman for her beloved, songs of scorn and religious songs; Alfonso X the Wise was among its patrons. Galician was by far the most-used language of charters in the 13th to 15th centuries. The Codex Calixtinus and the Historia Compostellana came from the kingdom's thriving 12th century, and its chalice, first given to the King of Galicia in the English armorial Segar's Roll of 1282, became the modern coat of arms of Galicia.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ferdinand III's accession in 1230 began a gradual decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>'s weight in the state. The court left Compostela, the kingdom lay on the periphery of a realm ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, and Alfonso X began a process that led, in the 15th and later centuries, to the replacement of Galician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, abbots and noblemen by Castilians. The withdrawal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gaunt">John of Gaunt</a>'s armies in 1388 ended the nobles' and towns' attempts to secede from the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, and in the 15th century, with no king visiting before the Catholic Monarchs in 1486, the kingdom broke into rival fiefdoms. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-hermandad">Santa Hermandad</a> and the Real <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> of 1500 brought it de facto under Castile; from about 1500 Castilian officials replaced the Galician elites and the Galician language was progressively discriminated against.</p><p>The wars of Philip II and his successors weighed on Galicia: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> from 1562, the English raids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Francis Drake</a> in 1589, and, by the count of the kingdom's chronicler, more than 68,000 men served the crown between 1624 and 1659. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> the kingdom was governed by a Captain General within the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Its Junta declared itself sovereign on 18 June 1808 and governed until the French conquest of 1809. The kingdom and its Junta lasted until the State Liberal Reform of 1833 and its provincial division; an uprising in 1846 restored Galicia's territorial unity for twenty-four days, but never the status of a kingdom.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, formed in 910 in the north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, was a separate realm only in brief interludes and otherwise shared its kings with León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. Its pilgrim shrine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>, its nobles' revolts and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galician-portuguese">Galician-Portuguese</a> poetry made it a force in medieval Iberia until Castilian centralisation reduced it to a province, dissolved in 1833.</em></p>

